.TH CVDBSET 8 "November 2019" "Xsan File System"
.SH NAME
cvdbset \- A program to control cvdb tracing.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
.nh
.HP
.B cvdbset
.RI [ options ]
.ad
.hy
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBcvdbset\fP is a tool
for system administrators to control
.BR cvdb (8)
tracing
information from the \fIXsan File System\fP
client file system.
.PP
The level of tracing emitted can be controlled on a per module basis. The
set of modules for which tracing is enabled is called the trace set. The 
level of tracing can be refined further by specifying a set of tracepoints 
(such as entry/exit points). The set of enabled tracepoints is called the
logmask.
.PP
Warning: enabling tracing can have a substantial performance impact.
.PP
\fBcvdbset\fP can be used to:
.RS
.PP 
List all the current client modules in the trace set.
.PP
Add all modules to the trace set.
.PP
Define the trace set.
.PP
Add selected modules to trace set
.PP
Remove selected modules from the trace set
.PP
Set the logmask for a set of modules in the trace set.
.PP
Resize the logging buffer
.PP
Start/stop continuous tracing
.PP
Disable tracing
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.IP "no options"
Display the whether tracing is enabled/disabled, the size of the logging buffer,
the modules in the trace set, and their corresponding logmasks.
.IP \fBall\fR
Enable tracing of all modules.  Once cvdbset with a list of modules is 
invoked, some modules are turned off.  \fBcvdbset all\fR sets all modules
for tracing.  When used with \fB+\fR or \fB-\fR, add or remove all modules.
.IP "[\fB:\fR]\fImodule1\fR [\fB:\fR]\fImodule2\fR ..."
When invoked with a list of modules, cvdbset first disables all modules.
Then, it enables exactly the given list of modules. To see all modules that
can be enabled, use the \fBcvdbset -l\fR command/option. If the module name is
preceded by a \fB:\fR, all modules containing the module name will be affected.
.IP "\fB+ \fR[\fB:\fR]\fImodule1 \fR[\fB:\fR]\fImodule2 \fR..."
When invoked with a plus sign (+) as the first argument followed by a
list of modules, the given list of modules is added to the
current trace set. If the module name is preceded by a \fB:\fR, all modules
containing the module name will be affected.
.IP "\fB\- \fR[\fB:\fR]\fImodule1 \fR[\fB:\fR]\fImodule2\fR ..."
When invoked with a minus sign (-) as the first argument followed by a
list of modules, the given list of modules is removed from the
current trace set.  If the module name is preceded by a \fB:\fR, all modules
containing the module name will be affected.
.IP \fB-h\fR
Display a help message and exit.
.IP \fB-c\fR
Enable continuous cvdb tracing. The trace log will be retrieved once
per second and placed in files named cvdbout.000001, ...
.IP \fB-d\fR
Disable cvdb tracing. 
.IP \fB-g\fR
Dump the current trace buffer to standard out.
.IP \fB-l\fR
Display whether logging is enabled, the buffer size, and the logmask for all 
modules.
.IP \fB-L\fR
Display the list of all available tracepoints for use with the -t option.
.IP "\fB-r\fP \fImb\fP"
Resize the trace buffer to \fImb\fR megabytes.
.IP "\fB-t\fP \fItracepoint\fP"
For the indicated modules, enable tracing only for the indicated 
tracepoints. Multiple \fB-t\fR options can be supplied. Use the
\fB-L\fR option to \fBcvdbset\fR to see a listing of tracepoints.
