.Ce
.PP
To add rwbuf and vnops modules to the current tracing set with the cvENTRY 
and cvEXIT tracepoints enabled:
.IP
.B cvdbset -t cvENTRY -t cvEXIT + rwbuf vnops
.PP
This displays the following output:
.Cs
Adding rwbuf.
Adding vnops.
cvdb -m modules=0x0000000804000000 logmask=0x0000000000000003
.Ce
To remove rwbuf and vnops from the current tracing set:
.IP
.B cvdbset - rwbuf vnops
.PP
This displays the following output:
.Cs
Clearing rwbuf.
Clearing vnops.
cvdb -m modules=0x0000000804000000 logmask=0x0000000000000000
.Ce
.PP 
The special module \fBall\fP can be used with both the \fB+\fP and 
\fB-\fP options to add/remove all modules from the trace.
.PP
After tracing is enabled, \fBcvdbset -g\fP can be used to retrieve the trace.
When desired, \fBcvdbset -d\fP can be used to disable tracing.
.PP
Various
.BR cvdb (8)
command/options can be used for even finer
control of tracing. See
.BR cvdb (8)
for more details.
.SH "I/O PERFORMANCE ANALYSIS"
.PP
The 'perf' trace module is very useful to analyze I/O performance, for example:
.IP
.B cvdbset perf
.PP
Then \fBcvdbset -g\fR will display info like this:
.Cs
     PERF: Device Write 41 MB/s IOs 2 exts 1 offs 0x0 len 0x400000 mics 95589 ino 0x5
     PERF: VFS Write EofDmaAlgn 41 MB/s offs 0x0 len 0x400000 mics 95618 ino 0x5
.Ce
.PP
The 'PERF: Device' trace shows throughput measured for the device I/O.  It also shows the number of I/O's that it was broken into and number of extents (sequence of consecutive filesystem blocks).
        
The 'PERF: VFS' trace shows throughput measured for the read or write system call and significant aspects of the I/O including:
.Cs
    Dma  - DMA
    Buf  - Buffered
    Eof  - File extended
    Algn - Well formed DMA I/O
    Shr  - File is shared by another client
    Rt   - File is real time
    Zr   - Hole in file was zeroed
.Ce
.PP
Both traces also report file offset, I/O size, latency (mics), and inode number.
.PP
Sample use cases:
.IP "1) Verify I/O properties are as expected." +0.3i
The VFS trace can be used to ensure that the displayed properties are consistent with expectations, for example, well formed, buffered vs. DMA, shared/non-shared, or I/O size.  If a small I/O is being performed DMA then performance will be poor.  If DMA I/O is not well formed then it requires an extra data copy and may even be broken into small chunks.  Zeroing holes in files has a performance impact.
.IP "2) Determine if metadata operations are impacting performance." +0.3i
If VFS throughput is inconsistent or significantly less than Device throughput then it may be caused by metadata operations.  In that case it would be useful to display 'fsmtoken', 'fsmvnops', and 'fsmdmig' traces in addition to 'perf'.
.IP "3) Identify disk performance issues." +0.3i
If Device throughput is inconsistent or less than expected then it may indicate a slow disk in a storage pool or that RAID tuning is necessary.
.IP "4) Identify file fragmentation." +0.3i
If the extent count 'exts' is high then it may indicate a fragmentation problem.  This causes the device I/O's to broken into smaller chunks which can significantly impact throughput.
.IP "5) Identify read/modify/write condition." +0.3i
If buffered VFS writes are causing Device reads then it may be beneficial to match I/O request size to a multiple of the 'cachebufsize' (default 256KB or 1024K, see
.BR mount_acfs (8)).
Another way to avoid this is by truncating the file before writing.
